# What `vercel deploy` must not upload.
#
# .gitignore is NOT consulted for deploy uploads — backend.zip was listed there
# and was still sent, failing the deploy on Vercel's 100 MB limit. Because a
# .vercelignore is used *instead of* .gitignore, this file has to repeat
# everything .gitignore excluded rather than only adding to it. In particular
# .env* must stay listed: those files hold DATABASE_URL, CRON_SECRET and
# SESSION_SECRET, and dropping the line would ship them into the deployment
# source. That is the one rule here worth guarding.
#
# ---- this file got shorter when the app moved ----------------------
# It used to exclude backend/, db/backups/, db/live_snapshot.sql and a 283 MB
# backend.zip, because the deploy uploaded the WHOLE repository. The Next.js
# app now lives in frontend/ and `vercel deploy` runs from here, so the upload
# root is this directory — everything above it is out of reach and needs no
# rule. The exclusions were not relaxed; the directory being uploaded shrank.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing:
#   · the Vercel project's Root Directory must be `frontend`, or a dashboard
#     deploy still uploads the repo root and this file is never consulted;
#   · adding anything large to frontend/ DOES need a rule here again.

# --- Node / Next.js ------------------------------------------------------
node_modules
.next
out
build
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
.vercel

# --- Secrets & local env -------------------------------------------------
# Keep. .env.local sits in this directory and holds live credentials.
# There is deliberately no `!.env.example` negation any more: the template
# stayed at the repository root, where docker-compose documents it, so a
# negation here would name a file that does not exist.
.env*
*.pem

# --- Runtime artifacts ---------------------------------------------------
captures/
*.log

# --- OS / editor ---------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
